Knowing how surgery works helps you decide more calmly. Here are the three main ways I work on the upper limb, in everyday words.

# Arthroscopy

The joint is entered through two or three small portals a few millimetres wide. A camera goes through one, instruments through the others. The inside of the shoulder is seen magnified on a screen and the work is done without opening the muscles.

I use it for most cuff repairs, for stabilisations, for calcific tendinitis, stiffness and labral tears. The advantage is not only cosmetic: less pain in the first weeks, lower infection risk, earlier rehabilitation.

# Open surgery

Some problems are best solved open: displaced fractures to fix with plates, reconstructions with bone graft, tendon transfers, replacements. The incision is wider but the procedure is more direct and, for these indications, safer.

It is not "old" surgery. It is the right surgery for those cases.

# Replacement

A shoulder replacement substitutes the worn joint surfaces. **Anatomic** when the cuff works, **reverse** when it no longer does or in many fractures of the elderly.

I plan the operation on a 3D reconstruction of the CT scan, choosing sizes and positions before entering theatre. In some cases I use patient-specific printed guides. In theatre, the plan becomes decisions already made.

# Before and after

**Before.** Pre-operative visit with the anaesthetist, blood tests, ECG if needed. I explain what will happen, what to bring, what to expect.

**After.** A sling for a period that depends on the procedure. A written exercise programme. Scheduled follow-ups. Your physiotherapist receives precise instructions.

# Who decides

You, with the right information. My job is to tell you honestly what I expect from each path, including the path of not operating.
